 Top of the Morning 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Yesterday afternoon a source called to ask about timing for a story we had discussed. "Tomorrow," I replied. "Unless something really big happens."

 Just hours later, the source texted: "Looks like you have bigger news to cover." Driving the news: Nvidia co-founder and CEO Jensen Huang admitted to having a romantic relationship with an alleged Chinese spy, apologizing to his family and company employees.

 The company's board said it would launch an independent investigation, adding that it retains confidence in Huang and his leadership team. Nvidia shares opened nearly 7% lower, wiping out hundreds of billions of dollars in market value. Why it matters: This could be catastrophic for the company, the tech industry, and the country.

 Nvidia underlies the entire AI trade, which already was under pressure that ratcheted up after last week's midterm elections. Not just in terms of its market-leading chips, but also in terms of the money those chips generate — which often gets fed back into the flywheel via strategic and venture capital dealmaking. Just this morning in South Korea, Nvidia announced new investments in South Korean startups. The big picture: Almost any other CEO would already have been axed. If not for the national security risks, then for the lies that may result in a pretty compelling shareholder lawsuit.

 But no one yet can imagine separating Huang from Nvidia. His black leather jacket might as well be the company's logo. He is both structurally and spiritually integral to Nvidia, a company whose flat org chart doesn't suggest a succession plan. In short, Huang may be too big to fire. Zoom in: There are a ton of questions still to be answered, both by the board investigation and the inevitable congressional hearings.

 Most will be for Huang. But also many for White House officials like Secretary of State Marco Rubio and Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick. There is reporting that the White House has been aware of Huang's affair for months, yet continued to let him attend presidential events and travel with presidential delegations. It also was deferential to Huang when it came to AI policy, including on chip sale agreements to China. President Trump yesterday shrugged off the controversy, even saying he "would certainly take" Huang on next week's trip to China. But that's not likely to satisfy Congress, including Republicans who may be more emboldened than ever to buck the administration. The bottom line: AI is much bigger than any individual, but Huang is as close as the industry can get to a key man.

 
 
